IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cccfa-types`

-- Credit Contracts and Consumer Finance Act 2003 — GOAL 2: INTEREST AND
-- HIGH-COST CREDIT.
--
--   "What is the most interest that may be charged; on what may default
--    interest run; is the contract high-cost, may it be entered into, and
--    how much more may be charged?"
--
-- Source: ../registers/source-bundle/act_public_2003_52.txt — ss 5 (daily
-- interest rate), 39, 40, 45C, 45E-45I.

§ `Goal 2 — interest and high-cost credit`

-- s 39 "(1) The maximum amount of an interest charge ... is,— (a) in the case
-- of 1 annual interest rate ..., the amount determined by applying the daily
-- interest rate to the unpaid daily balances". s 5: "daily interest rate
-- means the rate determined by dividing the annual interest rate by 365".
@ref ss 5, 39(1)(a)
GIVEN rate IS A NUMBER
      balances IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the most interest at an annual rate of` rate `on the daily balances` balances MEANS
    sum balances TIMES rate DIVIDED BY 365

-- s 39 "(2) However, an interest charge ... for a week, a fortnight, a month,
-- a quarter of a year, or a half of a year may be determined by applying the
-- annual interest rate or rates, divided by 52 (for a week), by 26 (for a
-- fortnight), by 12 (for a month), by 4 (for a quarter of a year), or by 2
-- (for a half of a year), to the whole or that part of the average [daily
-- balance]".
@ref s 39(2)
GIVEN periods IS A NUMBER, rate IS A NUMBER, average IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the interest for one of` periods `periods a year at an annual rate of` rate `on an average balance of` average MEANS
    IF   periods EQUALS 52 OR periods EQUALS 26 OR periods EQUALS 12 OR periods EQUALS 4 OR periods EQUALS 2
    THEN rate DIVIDED BY periods TIMES average
    ELSE `s 39(2) allows only weekly, fortnightly, monthly, quarterly or half-yearly periods`

`s 39(2) allows only weekly, fortnightly, monthly, quarterly or half-yearly periods` MEANS
    REFUSE "s 39(2) applies only to a week, a fortnight, a month, a quarter or a half of a year"

-- s 40 "(2) ... a consumer credit contract may provide for a differential
-- rate if the higher rate is imposed only— (a) in the event of a default in
-- payment, in respect of the amount of the default, and while the default
-- continues; or (b) in the event of the debtor causing the credit limit ...
-- to be exceeded and while the credit limit is exceeded. ... (2B) The higher
-- rate ... may not be imposed on any amount that becomes payable earlier"
-- because of the default, unless the contract is an on demand facility
-- (s 40(2A)(c)).
@ref s 40(2)-(2C)
GIVEN f IS A `Default interest facts`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the amount on which default interest may run` f MEANS
        f's `amount in default that was due anyway`
    PLUS f's `amount over the credit limit`
    PLUS (IF f's `an on demand facility` THEN f's `amount made payable early because of the default` ELSE 0)

-- s 45C "high-cost consumer credit contract means ... (a) a contract that
-- provides for an annual interest rate of 50% or greater: (b) ... the weighted
-- average annual interest rate ... is, or is likely to be, 50% or greater on
-- any day ...: (c) ... the total rate of the interest charges (including
-- default interest charges) that may be applied cumulatively ... is, or is
-- likely to be, a rate of 50% or greater: (d) a contract of a class declared
-- by the FMA".
@ref s 45C
@export Whether a consumer credit contract is a high-cost consumer credit contract under s 45C of the Credit Contracts and Consumer Finance Act 2003 (NZ): an annual interest rate, weighted average rate, or cumulative default or over-limit rate of 50% or more, or a class the FMA has declared. DRAFT, not reviewed by a New Zealand lawyer.
GIVEN h IS A `High-cost facts`  @desc The contract's rates (percent a year) and any FMA declaration
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a high-cost consumer credit contract` h IF
        h's `annual interest rate, percent` AT LEAST 50
    OR  h's `highest weighted average annual rate, percent` AT LEAST 50
    OR  h's `highest cumulative rate on default or over-limit, percent` AT LEAST 50
    OR  h's `of a class declared by the FMA`

-- s 45F "(1) No creditor (C) may enter into a high-cost consumer credit
-- contract with— (a) a debtor who has an unpaid balance on any high-cost
-- consumer credit contract under which the creditor is a person other than
-- C; or (b) a person who has had, at any time within the preceding 15 days,
-- an unpaid balance [likewise]". s 45G "(1) No creditor (C) may enter into a
-- high-cost consumer credit contract with a debtor who has entered into 2 or
-- more high-cost consumer credit contracts at any time within the preceding
-- 90 days." (2) of each: the defence of reasonable inquiries and grounds.
-- "Within the preceding n days" of day D: from D − n to D − 1 (fork F3).
-- s 45I: compound interest is prohibited.
@ref ss 45C, 45F, 45G, 45I
@export Whether a creditor may enter into a high-cost consumer credit contract on a day under ss 45F, 45G and 45I of the Credit Contracts and Consumer Finance Act 2003 (NZ): not with a debtor who has, or had in the preceding 15 days, an unpaid balance on another creditor's high-cost contract, nor one who entered into 2 or more high-cost contracts in the preceding 90 days (unless the creditor made reasonable inquiries and had reasonable grounds), and never on terms providing for compound interest. A contract that is not high-cost is not limited by these sections. DRAFT, not reviewed by a New Zealand lawyer.
GIVEN h IS A `High-cost facts`  @desc The rates, the debtor's other high-cost contracts and balances, the defence, and compound interest
      d IS A DATE  @desc The day the contract would be entered into
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the high-cost contract may be entered into` h `on` d IF
        NOT `is a high-cost consumer credit contract` h
    OR  (    NOT h's `provides for compound interest`
         AND (    h's `the creditor has the s 45F or 45G defence`
              OR (    NOT h's `has an unpaid balance with another high-cost creditor`
                  AND NOT (CONSIDER h's `last day of a balance with another high-cost creditor`
                           WHEN JUST last THEN last AT LEAST Date (Day d MINUS 15)
                           WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE)
                  AND sum (map (GIVEN x YIELD IF x AT LEAST Date (Day d MINUS 90) AND x LESS THAN d THEN 1 ELSE 0) (h's `high-cost contracts entered on`)) LESS THAN 2)))

-- s 45E "(1) The maximum costs of borrowing that are recoverable under a
-- high-cost consumer credit contract and all related consumer credit
-- contracts is an amount equal to the first advance." Its own example: a $100
-- first advance, $32 already charged, leaves $68.
@ref s 45E(1)
GIVEN h IS A `High-cost facts`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the further costs of borrowing recoverable under` h MEANS
    max 0 (h's `first advance` MINUS h's `costs of borrowing so far across related contracts`)

-- s 45H "(1) The maximum rate of charge that is recoverable under a high-cost
-- consumer credit contract is 0.8% per day, calculated in accordance with
-- the regulations as a proportion of the amount of credit provided." Read as
-- 0.8% of the credit provided for each day (fork F4).
@ref s 45H(1)
GIVEN credit IS A NUMBER, term IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the most charge at 0.8% a day on credit of` credit `for` term `days of credit` MEANS
    0.008 TIMES credit TIMES term
